#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# dev/stabilize-record — the one script through which a stabilization run's records
# reach the repository, and the one place its state is read back and decided
# (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-05, "The stabilization workflow, as ruled": rulings 4, 5, 6,
# 9, 10, 12, 13 and 16; the builders' choices are the two entries of the same date, "The
# record script, as built" and "The record script reads the run back", and the three
# entries of 2026-10-06, "The record script holds what the harness reads its work from",
# "The run's decision table, settled" and "The decision table's holes, closed" — and, for
# what a clause's status is, "Evidence belongs to a test-set item"; for the counters, the
# exits and where a write is taken, "Every loop has a counter on record"; and, for what
# that status is computed FROM, the entry of 2026-10-07, "The derivation's inputs are held
# where they are written").
#
# A stage's reporters have a shell and no write tool, and what they write is public on
# the next push and read by fences on the next gate. So neither an agent nor the harness
# writes a report, a ledger row or an item's result by hand: this script decides the path,
# replaces host paths, stops on the public-hygiene scan and writes atomically — or
# refuses in one line and writes nothing.
#
# And the harness is a script with no file system and no shell: it sees what an agent
# returns to it. So the run's state leaves this script as ONE JSON document (state), which
# an agent relays and never compiles; and what follows from that state — whether a finding
# is inside the round's test set, where it is routed, what the round does next — is
# computed here, where a suite holds it to a truth table, and nowhere else.
#
# One run's files, all under completions/artifacts/<run>/:
#
#   opening.md                              the opening record — the human-led step's, never
#                                           this script's
#   run.md                                  the run's facts: its stop mode, its two bounds,
#                                           the previous release, the default scope and
#                                           the clauses of its closing condition (run-set)
#                                           — and the census of the ledger, which this
#                                           script counts (ledger-add, result-set)
#   ledger.md                               one row per finding          (ledger-add, ledger-set,
#                                                                         triage-set: the grade,
#                                                                         result-set: a red run)
#   clauses.md                              one row per clause — RENDERED from the results,
#                                           and written by no caller     (THE CLAUSE TABLE)
#   bounds.md                               one row per declared bound   (bound-set)
#   test-set.md                             one row per instrument item  (item-set)
#   r<N>/scope.md                           the round's doors            (scope-set)
#   r<N>/triage.md                          the round's triage record    (triage-set)
#   r<N>/round.md                           the round record: its facts  (round-set) — and
#                                           what this script counts in the round: the runs
#                                           of each item (result-set), the findings its
#                                           triage left (triage-set), the attempts of a
#                                           stage that reached their record (apply)
#   r<N>/results.md                         one row per run of an item the round's scope
#                                           selected                     (result-set)
#   r<N>/gate.md                            the candidate's gate: what the full gate
#                                           showed red before the round's records were
#                                           written                      (gate-set)
#   r<N>/checks/<item>.a<A>.json            a held check's verdict, as dev/stabilize-step
#                                           judged its command — written once, with the
#                                           result of that run           (result-set)
#   .pending.json                           the journal (below) — never committed
#   r<N>/reports/test/<reporter>.a<A>.md    a test-stage report          (report)
#   r<N>/reports/fix/c<K>/<reporter>.a<A>.md  a fix-stage report, cycle K  (report)
#
# A RUN IS OPENED when completions/artifacts/<run>/ is a directory holding a regular
# file opening.md. This script creates neither, so a mistyped run name is refused
# (not-opened) and never mints a second run directory.
#
# Identifiers. <run>, <reporter>, a ledger key, a cited bound, a clause, and a test-set
# item and its kind are slugs
# — a-z0-9 words joined by single `-`, at most 100 characters, the key grammar of
# completions/artifacts/M55/seed-ledger.md. A round, a cycle and an attempt are
# positive integers without a leading zero. Anything else is refused (bad-id) before a
# path is built, and a link at any step below the run directory is refused (outside).
#
# Usage:
#
#   dev/stabilize-record report --run R --round N --stage test|fix [--cycle K]
#                        --reporter S --attempt A [--scratch DIR]... <report
#   dev/stabilize-record report … --from FILE
#       The report on stdin — or, with --from, THE FILE its reporter wrote it to, and
#       then stdin is not read (A WRITER FED FROM A FILE, below) — written to the path
#       above; prints that path. --cycle is the fix stage's and only its. A path already taken is refused (exists): a
#       re-run passes the next attempt and the stale report stays where it is.
#       A REPORT ENDS WITH THE LINE
#           <!-- end of report -->
#       as its last line, and it stays in the file. A report without it was cut off on
#       its way — the command it was piped from died — or was never finished, and is
#       refused (truncated): a short text is still text, and nothing else tells the two
#       apart. Input that is JSON needs no such line: cut off, it is not JSON.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record ledger-add --run R [--scratch DIR]... <rows.json
#       One JSON object per finding, or an array of them:
#         key, doctype (jigc-feedback | inconsistency), round (0 is the opening),
#         source, door, clause, repro            — required; the last four are text
#         grade, graded_by, bound, disposition, detail — optional, as for ledger-set
#       A key the ledger already holds is not appended again and keeps its row: a
#       finding found again inherits it. Prints {"ledger", "appended", "existing"}.
#       THE LEDGER'S CENSUS: every key a call adds is named in the run's own record
#       (run.md: findings), in the same batch. A row the census names is a row the ledger
#       has, whoever deleted it: `state` and every writer of the ledger refuse (corrupt) a
#       ledger that lacks one, or holds a row the census does not name.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record ledger-set --run R [--scratch DIR]... <patches.json
#       One JSON object per row, or an array: key, and a grade or a disposition.
#         grade        ungraded | breaks | unclear | no-break | out-of-scope
#                      | needs-bound | confirmed | regression | refuted
#         graded_by    triage      gives breaks, unclear, no-break, out-of-scope and
#                                  needs-bound (out of scope as triage reads it, under
#                                  no declared bound: the finding goes to the human)
#                      verify-real gives confirmed, regression (confirmed, and green on
#                                  the previous release), refuted
#                      human       gives any of them; ungraded has no grader
#         bound        with out-of-scope and only with it: the declared bound it cites.
#                      It is a row of bounds.md, or the whole batch is refused
#                      (no-such-bound) — here, in ledger-add and in triage-set.
#         disposition  open | fixed | admitted | bound | later | no-action
#         detail       fixed: the fix's sha · bound: its reach · admitted, later,
#                      no-action: a note, optional · open: none
#       A key the ledger lacks is refused (no-such-row). Prints {"ledger", "updated"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record result-set --run R --round N --commit SHA [--scratch DIR]...
#                        <results.json
#       WHAT AN ITEM OF THE TEST SET DID, in the round whose scope selected it — one JSON
#       object per item, or an array of them, all of runs on the commit SHA (a full sha):
#         item         a row of the test set (no-such-row), which round N's scope selects
#                      (state: WHETHER THE LATEST ROUND RUNS AN ITEM — the same rule, of
#                      round N's doors). An item no scope selected owes nothing and has
#                      no result: it is refused (bad-value).
#         outcome      green | red | void — it ran to its end and showed nothing red; it ran
#                      to its end and is red (a deterministic check); it was not reached, or
#                      did not run to its end
#         reason       with void and only with it: why, in a line of text
#         verdict      FOR A HELD CHECK, AND ONLY FOR ONE — an item whose kind is
#                      `held-<id>` (item-set): the file dev/stabilize-step kept its
#                      verdict of the held command in (<scratch>/hold/<name>/verdict.json).
#                      Such a row names the item and this, AND NOTHING ELSE: its outcome
#                      is the verdict the tool read out of the command's own output, and
#                      no word is taken from a caller (A SCRIPTED CHECK'S RESULT, below)
#                      — BUT `void`, for a held check that left no verdict to name: the
#                      row is then {item, outcome: void, reason}, its reason a word.
#         doctype, door, repro   with red and only with it: A RED RUN IS A FINDING. Its row
#                      is appended to the ledger by this call, under the key `red-<item>`,
#                      ungraded and open — round N, the item's clause, and these three as a
#                      ledger row has them — so that the next triage grades it and it is
#                      routed, ruled or fixed like any other. A key the ledger holds keeps
#                      its row, as a finding found again does; but a row that says `fixed`
#                      is OPEN again: the item is red after its fix.
#       THE ATTEMPT IS COMPUTED, never supplied: an item's runs in a round are numbered from
#       1. While round N is NOT TESTED, a result is the run of its test stage, and an item
#       has one (exists). Once it IS tested, a result is A RE-RUN — an attempt inside the
#       round that selected the item, never a round of its own — and is taken only where
#       THE STATE ASKS FOR IT: its `next` is `retest`, and the item is one it lists as due
#       in that round (state: A CLAUSE'S STATUS IS DERIVED). Any other re-run is refused
#       (bad-value). A round with no scope is refused (no-scope).
#       Prints {"results", "recorded": [{item, attempt, outcome}], "findings"} — the path,
#       what was recorded, and the ledger keys this call filed or opened again.
#       HOW MANY RUNS OF EACH ITEM THE ROUND HOLDS is counted into the round's own record
#       by the same call (round.md: ran, as `<item> a<K>`): a result that is gone later —
#       an item's last one, or its only one — is missed there, and `state` refuses (corrupt)
#       results that hold fewer runs of an item, or more, than the record counts.
#
#       A SCRIPTED CHECK'S RESULT IS THE TOOL'S VERDICT (the second repair plan of the
#       `test` half, K10). A check that is one long command — the gate, the regression
#       set — is started, waited for and JUDGED by dev/stabilize-step (its `hold-start`
#       and `hold-wait`); what an agent would return of it is a word, and a word can be
#       wrong. So an item of kind `held-<id>` has no outcome a caller could name
#       (bad-value), and no other item has a verdict's file (bad-value). The file is ONE
#       line that ends with the sha256 of itself, and it is that tool's (`tool`), of a
#       held `<id>` (`kind`), green, red or void with why — and, where the command was
#       asked for a commit, of THIS call's commit; anything else is refused (bad-value)
#       and nothing is written. green is green; void is void, its reason the verdict's
#       `why` and its sentence; RED IS A FINDING like every red run — filed under
#       `red-<item>`, its door `the held check <item>`, its repro the verdict's own
#       sentence and where the verdict lies.
#       A HELD CHECK THAT LEFT NO VERDICT IS VOID BY A WORD, AND NOTHING ELSE IS A WORD.
#       A held command can end with no verdict's file to hand in — its job is dead, its
#       start was refused, the stage ended while it ran, a gate is not held again off the
#       candidate's tree — and an item with no result stays due for a re-run nobody can
#       record: a state no stage leaves. So `void`, and ONLY `void`, is taken as a word
#       for a held item, and its reason is A WORD OF A DECLARED SHAPE, never a sentence:
#       `<word>` or `<word>:<key>` — lowercase a-z0-9 words joined by single `-`, and a
#       key of plain characters (A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -), which is the held job's name; 200
#       characters at most (bad-value). A shape and no list of words: the words are the
#       step tool's and the harness's own (dead, running, taken, no-verdict, ...), and a
#       list here would be a second copy of theirs. `green` and `red` stay the tool's
#       verdict or nothing (bad-value, as before). The row's detail is `without a
#       verdict: <reason>`, no verdict is written on record for that attempt, and `state`
#       refuses one that lies there (corrupt). IT IS VOID LIKE ANY ITEM'S: its clause is
#       void, it forbids `close`, it is owed its re-run where the state asks for one, and
#       after two runs its clause is the human's. AND THE VERDICT REACHES THE RECORD AS A FILE
#       WRITTEN ONCE, in the same batch as the result: r<N>/checks/<item>.a<attempt>.json
#       — the item, its attempt, the round and the commit, the kind, the verdict, why,
#       what was asked, the exit status, the facts and the evidence of the kind (for the
#       regression set its own line: the two commits and their binaries' hashes, the
#       list's path, commit and hash, every difference on and off the list and every
#       test that was excluded), host paths replaced as in every file of a run. `state`
#       holds every such result to its file — one that is gone, or says another word, is
#       refused (corrupt): what is deleted is missed — and `vet` holds the file to its
#       result (bad-value) and to a verdict's name (bad-id).
#
#       THE CLAUSE TABLE (clauses.md) IS RENDERED, AND NOTHING SETS A CLAUSE'S STATUS. It
#       is the view of the derivation below — a row per clause of the run's census (run-set:
#       clauses): the items that judge it and where each one's run stands, the commit and
#       the rounds of those runs, and green, red or void — and every writer whose table the
#       derivation reads renders it again in the same batch as its own write (run-set,
#       item-set, result-set, round-set). A table that differs from what the results give —
#       a status a hand changed, a row or the file deleted, a row added — is refused
#       (corrupt) by `state` and by each of those writers, naming what differs: it is
#       never read, and never quietly rendered over.
#       NOTHING REMOVES A CLAUSE, and nothing removes an item of the test set (item-set) —
#       nor what a round has run of one, or owes it: its row stands in the four cells the
#       derivation reads.
#       Dropping or replacing either is no ruling inside a round: it changes the closing
#       condition or the test set, and both are fixed by the run's human-led opening
#       before round 1 — a run whose condition changes is opened again. The one ruling a
#       clause that is still not green has a recorded form for is one more re-run of its
#       items (round-set: granted).
#
#   dev/stabilize-record bound-set --run R --bound B [--reach TEXT] [--ruling TEXT]
#                        [--pin TEXT] [--scratch DIR]...
#       Writes the declared bound's row, or updates the cells named. A bound is always
#       the human's ruling, so a row enters the list only as the record of one: a new
#       row needs its reach, where the human ruled it (--ruling: the opening record, a
#       stop's list and item), and the test that pins it or the word `unpinned` (--pin).
#       None of the three may be blank. Prints {"bounds", "bound"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record scope-set --run R --round N [--scratch DIR]... <scope.json
#   dev/stabilize-record scope-set … --from FILE
#       The round's doors, as the scope step resolved them — one JSON object, on stdin
#       or in the file --from names:
#         {"included": [DOOR...], "excluded": [DOOR...]}
#         DOOR = {"door", "registry", "derivation"}   — text, none of it blank: the door,
#                the registry it comes from, and how it was derived
#       Both sides are named, even when empty, and a door stands on one side, once.
#       Where a whole registry is inside, every door of it is listed. A round's test
#       set is written ONCE: a second scope for the round is refused (exists).
#       Prints {"scope", "included", "excluded"} — the path and the two counts.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record triage-set --run R --round N <entries.json
#       What the round's triage and the verifier established — one JSON object per
#       finding, or an array of them:
#         key          a row of the ledger (a finding is added before it is triaged)
#         grade        triage's: breaks | unclear | no-break | out-of-scope | needs-bound
#         bound        with out-of-scope and only with it: a row of bounds.md
#         verdict      the verifier's, of a finding graded breaks or unclear:
#                      confirmed | refuted. Without one, the finding awaits it.
#         regression   with confirmed and only with it: true when the repro is green on
#                      the previous release's binary, false when it is red there too —
#                      the verifier's fact. ABSENT, OR null, WHERE THE VERIFIER DOES NOT
#                      KNOW: it was handed no binary of the previous release, the door is
#                      one that release does not have, its return does not back the
#                      claim. The cell is then `-` beside `confirmed`, the finding is
#                      graded confirmed and routed as one, and `state` names it
#                      (regression_unknown): an unknown fact is never written as `no`.
#                      Anything else — a word, a number — is refused (bad-value).
#         fork         with a verdict and only with one: A FORK — the verifier found the
#                      finding to contest a settled decision, so what is done about it is
#                      the human's to rule and no fixer's. One object, all three named:
#                        kind   contested
#                        case   robust-now | cheap-cut-is-correct — the verdict of the
#                               robust-advocate's case
#                        drive  holds | differs — whether the independent drive of the
#                               advocate's proposal found what the advocate reported
#                      The case and the drive are the two reports of that fork among the
#                      stage's; this cell is what a later invocation reads them by.
#       NOTHING ELSE IS TAKEN. Whether the finding is INSIDE the round's test set is
#       computed here — its ledger row's door is one of the round's included doors, as
#       scope-set wrote them — and is never supplied; a round with no scope is refused
#       (no-scope). The entry's row in r<N>/triage.md says: inside | outside: excluded
#       | outside: unlisted, triage's grade, the verdict, the regression, the
#       disposition the ledger row carried when the round recorded it ("found with"),
#       and the fork (`<kind>: <case>, <drive>`, or `-`).
#       An entry recorded again replaces its row — that is how a verdict arrives later,
#       and how a round says that it found a finding again: only then is it re-recorded.
#       The ledger's grade follows from the same entry, so the two cannot disagree: the
#       verdict where there is one (regression, confirmed, refuted; by verify-real),
#       else triage's grade. A disposition is never touched: a finding found again
#       inherits its row's. Its files are ONE batch: all are written or none is, and
#       a call killed between them is finished by the next writer (the journal, below).
#       THE PASSES ARE COUNTED BY THIS CALL, never by an invocation and never supplied:
#       every finding the record LEAVES without a grade or a verdict — named by an entry or
#       not — is named in the round's own record (round.md: awaiting, as `<key> ungraded
#       <K>` or `<key> unverified <K>`), K being how many of the round's triage records IN
#       A ROW have left it for that reason. A finding the record settles is named no more,
#       and one that changes from ungraded to unverified starts at 1 (state: `awaits`).
#       Prints {"triage", "ledger", "recorded"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record item-set --run R [--scratch DIR]... <items.json
#       The run's test set, item by item — one JSON object per instrument item, or an
#       array of them:
#         item         the item's id                                   — a slug
#         kind         what kind of instrument it is: a review row, a trial arm, an
#                      audit area, a deterministic check. A slug, and NOT a vocabulary of
#                      this script's: the harness has a chain of agents per kind, and a
#                      kind it has none for is its halt, never a guess of either's. ONE
#                      FORM IS READ HERE: `held-<id>` is A HELD CHECK — a check that is
#                      one long command, which dev/stabilize-step starts, waits for and
#                      judges; <id> is the id of that tool's kind of held command
#                      (`held-gate`, `held-regression`). Its result is that tool's
#                      verdict and no caller's word (result-set), and its brief is for a
#                      reader: no agent runs a command off it.
#         clause       the clause of the closing condition it judges   — a slug, AND A
#                      CLAUSE OF THE CENSUS (run-set: clauses) once that is written: the
#                      derivation walks the census, so an item of a clause it does not
#                      name would judge nothing, and is refused (bad-value). While there is
#                      no census the row is taken, and the census is held to it instead.
#         runs         every-candidate | in-scope — on every candidate, or only in a
#                      round whose test set reaches it ("hunt once per change, check
#                      always")
#         brief        where its brief lies, or what it is             — text, not blank
#         doors        the doors it covers, each in the door's own words — an array of
#         registries   texts; and the registries it covers whole. Optional, but an
#                      in-scope item names at least one of either, or no round could
#                      ever reach it. An entry is one line, without `<br>`.
#       An item the test set holds is replaced whole — UNTIL A ROUND HAS RUN IT OR OWES IT
#       A RUN. From the first round whose results hold a run of it, or the first TESTED
#       round whose scope selects it, ITS CLAUSE, RUNS, DOORS AND REGISTRIES STAND: they
#       are the four cells the derivation reads (a list is the set it names, in any order),
#       and a row that would change one is refused (exists) — it would take a void run, or
#       what a round owes, out of every clause's status, and turn `retest` into `close`.
#       What is taken says what the legitimate ways are: the brief and the kind are
#       corrected at any time, by a row that names the four as they stand; an instrument
#       that covers something else is A NEW ITEM, under its own id, which the rounds that
#       reach it then owe a run; and what a round owes is removed by no write — a run whose
#       test set changes is opened again. Prints {"test_set", "written"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record round-set --run R --round N <facts.json
#       The round record's facts — one JSON object naming at least one of:
#         candidate    the commit the round tests, a full sha. A round whose record
#                      names its candidate is TESTED: its test stage was recorded.
#         base         the commit the round's change is measured from, a sha
#         binary       the sha256 of the one binary the candidate was driven as
#         cycles       how many fix -> audit cycles of the round are recorded: a
#                      positive integer, which never goes down
#         outcome      dropped | part — the round is OVER without having landed whole:
#                      it was dropped, or a part of it landed. A round that lands whole
#                      needs no word: it is over when nothing of it is open.
#         go           true — THE HUMAN'S GO after the stop that follows this round, in a
#                      run that stops after every round. Taken only while the state's
#                      `next` is `stop`, for that reason and that round; any other time it
#                      is refused (bad-value), so a go never lifts a stop nobody has seen.
#                      A GO ANSWERS ONE STOP: the record holds the count of fix cycles the
#                      round had when it was given (`c<K>`), and it stands while the round
#                      has that many. A round that is over AGAIN after one more fix cycle
#                      stops again, and takes its own go.
#         granted      the clause the human GRANTS ONE MORE RE-RUN, a slug: a clause
#                      with an item this round's scope selected whose re-run is spent
#                      and which is still not green. Taken only for a clause the state
#                      lists in `human_clauses`, in the round that selected such an item;
#                      else refused (bad-value). The record holds what it allows — the
#                      next attempt of each such item, as `<item> a<K>` — and a later
#                      grant adds to it: one grant, one more attempt.
#         reverify     the finding the human GRANTS ONE MORE TRIAGE, a ledger key: one
#                      the state's `human_list` names as still ungraded or unverified
#                      after its retry (`…-after-retry`). A fact of the latest round, whose
#                      record counts the passes; else refused (bad-value). The record
#                      holds the pass it allows, as `<key> p<K>`: one grant, one more.
#         again        test | fix — the stage the human GRANTS ONE MORE ATTEMPT: one the
#                      state's `human_stages` names, in the round it is spent in; else
#                      refused (bad-value). The record holds the attempt it allows, as
#                      `test a<K>` or `fix-c<C> a<K>`: one grant, one attempt.
#                      All four are the human's rulings: the harness writes them in the
#                      one step that records what the human ruled, and nowhere else —
#                      which is the harness's to hold, never this script's (WHAT THIS
#                      SCRIPT CANNOT KNOW, below).
#         cross-model       the items whose source pass was ALSO read by a model of
#         cross-model-void  another family in this round, and the items named for such a
#                      pass that did not get one — each an array of item ids, so that
#                      the use of that pass, and how much of it, shows at every stop
#       candidate, base, binary and outcome are written ONCE: a second, different
#       value is refused (exists). A candidate is taken for a round that has a scope
#       (no-scope), whose results are of runs on that commit, and whose gate on record —
#       where it holds one (gate-set) — is the gate of that commit (bad-value, each): a
#       round is tested over the doors its scope resolved, on one candidate, and THE
#       CANDIDATE'S GATE IS THE GATE OF THE CANDIDATE. EVERY FACT IS TAKEN
#       ONLY WHERE THE STATE ASKS FOR IT (WHERE THE STATE TAKES A WRITE, below) — and a fact
#       said again as it stands changes nothing and is taken anywhere. What the record
#       holds beside these — ran, awaiting, recorded — this script counts, and no caller
#       writes it. NOTHING ELSE IS TAKEN, and none of it is free text.
#       Prints {"round", "facts"} — the path, and every fact the record now holds.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record run-set --run R <facts.json
#       The run's facts, as its opening settled them — one JSON object naming at least
#       one of:
#         stop         every-round | at-the-bound — when the run stops for the human of
#                      its own accord: after every round, or only once the bound across
#                      rounds is reached and work remains. Written ONCE (exists): the
#                      other mode would take a stop at a spent bound away unseen.
#         rounds       the bound across rounds: how many rounds may have a FIX STAGE on
#                      record (state: the stop). A positive integer, which never goes
#                      down. Only the human raises it, and a raise is this call again —
#                      TAKEN ONLY AT ITS OWN STOP (`round-bound`): the raise is the go.
#         cycles       the bound inside a round: how many fix -> audit cycles a round may
#                      have (state: the stop). Three where it is not written; a number
#                      above the one that holds, raised only at ITS stop (`cycle-bound`).
#                      Either bound is also written while the run's opening is not done,
#                      or before any round is begun.
#         previous     the previous release's version (`1.2.3`, `1.2.3-rc.4`), and
#         previous-commit  the commit it was released from, a full sha: what the second
#                      binary of a round is built from, and what "a regression of the run"
#                      is measured against. Each is written ONCE (exists).
#         scope        delta | everything — the scope a round is started with when its
#                      invocation names none. Written ONCE (exists).
#         clauses      THE CENSUS: the clauses of the closing condition, a non-empty
#                      array of slugs. Written ONCE (exists) — it is what the run must
#                      satisfy, and what the clause table is held against: a clause the
#                      census names has a row, whoever deleted it. AND IT NAMES EVERY
#                      CLAUSE AN ITEM OF THE TEST SET ALREADY JUDGES, or the call is
#                      refused whole (bad-value): the clause is typed twice at an opening
#                      — here, and in every item's row — and the two are held to each
#                      other in both orders of writing (item-set).
#       THIS IS HOW THE OPENING'S MACHINE-READ FACTS GET IN. opening.md stays the
#       human-led step's prose, which this script neither writes nor reads; the step
#       that writes it calls run-set before its one commit. A run that lacks them is NOT
#       READY (state).
#       What the table holds beside these — findings, the ledger's census — this script
#       counts. NOTHING ELSE IS TAKEN, and none of it is free text. Prints {"run",
#       "facts"} — the path, and every fact.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record state --run R
#       The run's committed state, as one line of JSON; writes nothing. A run that was
#       never opened is an answer here, not a refusal: {"run", "opened": false}.
#       Otherwise:
#         run, opened
#         facts        {stop, rounds, cycles, previous, previous-commit, scope, clauses}
#                      as run-set wrote them (null where it did not; the bound on a
#                      round's cycles is three where it is null), and findings: the
#                      ledger's census
#         not_ready    what the opening still owes, or []: no-clause-row (the census names
#                      no clause, so the clause table has no row) · no-stop-mode ·
#                      no-round-bound (the mode is at-the-bound, and no number is written)
#                      · no-previous-release · no-default-scope
#         rounds       per round with a directory: {round, scope, triage, record —
#                      whether each file is there — facts: {candidate, base, binary,
#                      cycles, outcome, go, granted, reverify, again, cross-model,
#                      cross-model-void} as round-set wrote them (null where it did not;
#                      cycles 0, the lists empty; go: whether the go STANDS — it was given
#                      with as many fix cycles on record as the round has now) and {ran,
#                      awaiting, recorded} as this script counted them,
#                      results: [{item, attempt, commit, outcome,
#                      reason, finding}] as result-set wrote them, in the order they were
#                      recorded, reopened: the keys of the findings this
#                      round's triage found again, standing, with a fix on their row —
#                      the fixes that did not hold, counted where the round's stop shows
#                      them — test_reports, test_attempt, test_unrecorded, fix_cycles:
#                      [{cycle, reports, attempt, unrecorded}]}, the reports counted by
#                      file name, the attempt the highest one a report's name carries (0
#                      for none), and `unrecorded` how many attempts lie above the last
#                      one that reached its record (THE ATTEMPTS OF A STAGE, below)
#         round        the highest of them, or null
#         begins       the round a write may BEGIN — the one a `test` stage is handed that
#                      no directory holds yet — or null (WHERE THE STATE TAKES A WRITE)
#         doors        that round's {included, excluded}, each [{door, registry,
#                      derivation}] — or null before its scope is written
#         bounds       [{bound, reach, ruling, pin}]
#         clauses      [{clause, instrument, commit, scope, status, round, behind, retry,
#                      items}] — the clause table as it is derived (below)
#         items        [{item, kind, clause, runs, doors, registries, brief, selected}]
#                      — the test set, and whether the latest round runs each item
#                      (below); selected is null for an in-scope item while that round
#                      has no scope
#         never_selected  the ids of the items that run in-scope and that no TESTED round's
#                      scope has selected — before any round is tested, every such item
#         uncovered    [{door, registry, rounds}] — every door a round's scope INCLUDED
#                      that no item of the test set names, by the door or by the registry
#                      it comes from, with the rounds whose test set holds it
#                      (both: WHAT CANNOT BE HELD IS NAMED, below)
#         ledger       per row: key, doctype, round, source, door, clause, grade,
#                      bound, graded_by, disposition, detail, repro — and
#                        triage  the row's entry in the latest round that triaged it:
#                                {round, inside, door: included | excluded | unlisted,
#                                grade, bound, verdict, regression, found_with, fork:
#                                {kind, case, drive} or null}, or null
#                        reopened  whether its fix did not hold: it is open again
#                        route   fix | human | triage | recorded, and `why`
#                        awaits  null — or, for a finding the round's triage has not
#                                finished, {why: ungraded | unverified, passes: how many
#                                triage records of the latest round in a row left it so,
#                                retry: due | spent}
#         blockers     the keys routed fix
#         regression_unknown  the keys of the findings a verifier CONFIRMED WITHOUT
#                      KNOWING WHETHER EACH IS A REGRESSION (triage-set: regression absent
#                      or null), for as long as the finding is not routed recorded. Such
#                      a finding is routed by its grade and its door exactly as a
#                      confirmed non-regression is — fix inside the test set, the human's
#                      outside it — and is never fixed unasked as "a regression", which
#                      nobody established. The missing fact forbids `close` no longer
#                      than the finding itself does: it is an attribute of a finding,
#                      not a missing verdict.
#         human_list   [{key, why}] — the findings routed human
#         human_stages [{stage, round, cycle, at, attempt, attempts, why}] — the stage
#                      whose attempts are spent, when the step is that stage's: `attempt`
#                      is the one a grant would allow, `attempts` how many left no record
#         candidate    {round, commit, current} — the latest round that tested a
#                      candidate, the commit it tested, and whether that candidate is
#                      still the run's: false once a fix stage is on record in that round,
#                      or a later round is begun (null, null, false before any is tested)
#         fix_rounds   how many rounds have a fix stage on record: what the bound counts
#         untriaged    [{key, why}] — the findings routed triage
#         retest       the clauses an item of which is run again, when that is the step
#         human_clauses  [{clause, why}] — the clauses that are the human's   } once the
#         unsettled    [{cell, clauses}] — what the rulings do not settle      } clause
#                      table is all that is left to ask (below)
#         forbids_close  [{clause, status} | {"clauses": "no row"} | {finding, route}
#                      | {"candidate": "not-tested", round}]
#         next         not-ready | test | triage | rule | fix | retest | close | stop
#                      | unsettled
#         stop         null — or, when next is stop, {why: every-round | round-bound |
#                      cycle-bound, round, then}
#         position     {test, fix} — what an invocation of each stage would work on
#                      (below)
#       A cell is given as the table holds it, a `|` unescaped; `-` for none is null
#       where the cell is not free text. A table that is not this script's, a cell
#       outside its vocabulary, a triage record that disagrees with the doors it was
#       computed from, a clause table that is not the one the results give, a tested round
#       without a scope, a result whose earlier attempt is gone, a test set with an item
#       of a clause the census does not name, or a red result whose
#       finding the ledger lacks is refused (corrupt) — the state is never read on a guess.
#       AND WHAT IS DELETED IS MISSED, by the same refusal, naming what is gone: a ledger
#       row that the census, a triage record or a round's count of what its triage left
#       names; a result the round's record counts; the reports of an attempt the round's
#       record says reached its record. And a
#       run one of whose writes was killed between its files is refused (interrupted):
#       half a batch is never read as a finished one. An applied batch that no commit
#       holds yet is read like any working tree: it is what the files say.
#
#       THE ROUTE of a finding, a pure function of its row, the round's doors and the
#       bounds list — by its disposition first, then by its grade:
#         fixed                  recorded (fixed) — unless the latest round that triaged
#                                it found it again, standing, with that same fix on its
#                                row. The fix did not hold: the row does not inherit
#                                `fixed`, the finding is OPEN AGAIN (reopened) and is
#                                routed by its grade, as below. A later fix, or the
#                                human's ruling, is what records it.
#         bound, later           recorded (ruled): it carries the human's ruling
#         admitted               fix (admitted): the human admitted it to this run
#         open, no-action        human (fork) while the latest round that triaged it
#                                recorded A FORK on its entry (triage-set: fork) — whatever
#                                the verdict, wherever it was found: a contested fix is
#                                never a fixer's unasked. The human's ruling on it is one
#                                of the three above. Without a fork,
#                                by the grade — no-action is neither a fix nor a ruling:
#           ungraded             triage (ungraded)   — the round's triage is not
#           breaks, unclear      triage (unverified)   finished: never a fixer's, and
#                                never on the human's list as if it were verified —
#                                ONCE MORE, and then the human's: where the latest round's
#                                record counts two triage records in a row that left it so
#                                (awaiting), and the human granted no further pass
#                                (reverify), the route is human (ungraded-after-retry,
#                                unverified-after-retry). The human rules it — admitted,
#                                bound, later — or grants one more triage.
#           no-break, refuted    recorded (not-a-break)
#           out-of-scope         recorded (out-of-scope) while its bound is on the
#                                list; human (bound-not-listed) when it is not
#           needs-bound          human (needs-bound)
#           regression           fix (regression) — wherever it was found
#           confirmed            fix (inside) when the latest round that triaged it
#                                has its door among the included; human (outside)
#                                when not; human (untriaged) when no round placed it
#
#       THE CANDIDATE, AND WHEN A FIX STAGE IS ON RECORD. A round whose record names its
#       candidate is TESTED. A FIX STAGE IS ON RECORD in a round whose record counts a
#       fix -> audit cycle, or says how the fix stage ended it (its outcome). The
#       candidate the latest round tested is CURRENT — the run's own — for as long as no
#       fix stage is on record in that round; after that, what the round leaves is tested
#       by nobody until the next round's `test` stage is recorded. CLOSE IS REACHABLE ONLY
#       FROM A TESTED CANDIDATE: from a latest round that is tested, has no fix stage on
#       record, and left nothing open. (A dropped round lands nothing; it is read like
#       the others — the latest stage on record is a fix stage, and one more `test` round
#       is what confirms the candidate. A round's record does not say that its branch was
#       merged, only that a cycle was recorded: the state on a round's own branch reads
#       like the state after its landing.)
#
#       A CLAUSE'S STATUS IS DERIVED — from the census, the test set, and each TESTED
#       round's scope, results and record; no caller writes it, and the clause table is
#       its view. A round that is not tested yet changes nothing in it. Per clause:
#         items    the items of the test set that judge it, each as
#                    {item, runs, round, attempt, commit, outcome, reason, standing, why,
#                     rerun}
#                  round     the round that OWES the item: the latest tested round whose
#                            scope selects it (an every-candidate item: the latest tested
#                            round). null: no tested round's scope selected it — AN ITEM
#                            NO SCOPE HAS SELECTED OWES NOTHING, which is the scoped
#                            design ("hunt once per change"), not a gap.
#                  attempt, commit, outcome, reason
#                            its latest run in that round, as result-set recorded it —
#                            null where the round holds none
#                  standing  green | red — as that run says — or void, with `why`:
#                              void         the run did not run to its end
#                              not-run      the round selects the item and holds no run
#                                           of it: an instrument that never ran to its end
#                                           is never turned green by the items beside it
#                              not-current  a fix stage is on record in the latest tested
#                                           round: what it left is tested by nobody, so
#                                           nothing says which doors its fixes reach, and
#                                           a check's run is of an earlier candidate.
#                            — or not-selected, where `round` is null.
#                            So a HUNT'S run is current while no landed fix reaches its
#                            doors — the round after a fix stage says so by its scope: it
#                            selects the item, which then owes a run THERE, or it does
#                            not, and the earlier run stands — and a CHECK'S run is
#                            current only on the present candidate: every tested round
#                            selects it, so it owes a run in the latest one.
#                  rerun     null for an item that is green, not selected or not current
#                            (there is no candidate to run it on). Any other is owed its
#                            next attempt in its round:
#                              due    that attempt is the round's second — THE ONE RE-RUN
#                                     THAT IS AUTOMATIC — or its first, or one the human
#                                     granted (round-set: granted)
#                              spent  otherwise: the next one is the human's to grant
#         status   void while an item it is owed is void, and while it is owed no item at
#                  all; else red while one is red; else green — every item that judges it
#                  and that a tested round's scope selected has a green run that is current
#         instrument, commit, scope
#                  the view's cells: where each item stands, in words; the commit of the
#                  latest run among its owed items; the rounds that owe them
#         round    the round of that latest run, or null
#         behind   how far behind the current candidate that run is, IN FIX ROUNDS: how
#                  many rounds, from `round` on, have a fix stage on record. null where
#                  `round` is. (The unit is the one the committed record supports. In
#                  commits it is `git rev-list --count <commit>..<tip>`, which this script
#                  cannot say: it reads no git.)
#         retry    null for a green clause, and for one whose items are not current. Else
#                    no-instrument   no item of the test set judges the clause
#                    never-selected  items judge it, and no tested round's scope has
#                                    selected one: it has no evidence, and owes none
#                    spent           an item's rerun is spent
#                    due             otherwise: an item's rerun is due
#
#       THE NEXT STEP, a pure function of the routes, the clause table, the rounds'
#       records and the run's facts. A finding is OPEN while it is not routed recorded.
#       The latest round R is OVER when it is tested and its outcome is set or nothing is
#       open. The first that holds:
#         not-ready  the opening owes something (not_ready), and no stage starts
#         test       there is no round, or R is not tested: its `test` stage is run, or
#                    run again — or R is over and a fix stage is on record in it (its
#                    fixes landed, a part of it landed, or it was dropped): the next
#                    round's `test` stage comes first, WHATEVER THE CLAUSE TABLE SAYS
#         triage     a finding is routed triage: the round's triage is not finished. The
#                    stage that left it finishes it (the position, below) — grading,
#                    verification and the record, and no instrument run again
#         rule       a finding is routed human: the human's list has an unruled item — one
#                    found outside the test set, one that needs a bound, a fork, or one
#                    the round's triage could not settle in two passes
#         fix        a finding is routed fix
#       — and with nothing open, on a current candidate, by the clause table:
#         rule       a clause's retry is spent (human_clauses): the human may grant it
#                    one more re-run (round-set: granted), and no other ruling on a
#                    clause has a recorded form
#         unsettled  a clause's retry is `no-instrument` or `never-selected`
#         retest     a clause's retry is due: its due items are run again — AN ATTEMPT
#                    INSIDE THE ROUND THAT SELECTED THEM, never a round of its own, which
#                    counts against no bound and resolves no scope. `retest` names every
#                    such clause; one invocation runs the first one's due items of one
#                    round (the position, below).
#         close      every clause is green
#       THE ATTEMPTS OF A STAGE. Where the step is a stage's — test, triage, fix, retest —
#       and that stage has left reports and no record, in the directory it works in, as
#       many times in a row as anything is tried without the human (twice: its own attempt
#       and one more), the step is the human's instead: `next` is `rule`, `human_stages`
#       names the stage, and its position is refused (attempts-spent) until the human
#       grants one more attempt (round-set: again). (Only where the step IS that stage's:
#       a round whose last cycle left nothing open still has to land, whatever halted
#       before, and a bound that is spent is said first.) WHICH ATTEMPT REACHED ITS RECORD is a
#       fact this script writes: a batch (apply) that holds a stage's report check is the
#       record of that attempt, and names it in the round's record (recorded). An attempt
#       that left no report is not counted: it stopped before any reporter wrote, and its
#       halt went back to whoever invoked it.
#       THE STOP. The step above can be the human's first: `next` is then `stop`, and
#       `stop.then` is the step that follows the human's go.
#         every-round  the run's stop mode is every-round, R is over — whatever the step
#                      is — and R's record holds no `go` that stands. THE HUMAN'S GO IS A
#                      RECORDED FACT (round-set: go), AND IT ANSWERS ONE STOP: once it is
#                      written, `next` answers the step — until a fix cycle is recorded
#                      in R after it, where R, over again, stops again.
#         cycle-bound  the step is `fix`, and R's record counts as many fix -> audit
#                      cycles as the bound inside a round allows (facts.cycles, or
#                      three). The human's exits: the bound raised (run-set: cycles), the
#                      round left (round-set: outcome), or what is open ruled.
#         round-bound  the mode is at-the-bound, the step is `fix`, no fix stage is on
#                      record in R yet, and as many rounds have one as the bound allows
#                      (fix_rounds >= facts.rounds): one more fix round would start here.
#                      THE BOUND COUNTS FIX ROUNDS, NEVER TEST STAGES: the `test` stage
#                      after a landed round is always allowed — the last allowed fix round
#                      could not be confirmed otherwise — and so are a finished triage,
#                      the human's ruling on the list, and a clause's re-run. Only the
#                      human raises the bound, and the raise is the go (run-set).
#       Close is forbidden by every clause that is not green, by a census with no
#       clause, by every finding not routed recorded, and by a candidate that is not
#       tested — forbids_close lists them all.
#       UNSETTLED is not a decision: it names a state the recorded rulings do not
#       settle, and hands it to the human instead of guessing. The cells left, both with
#       nothing open on a current candidate: not-green-and-no-instrument (no item of the
#       test set judges the clause: there is no instrument to run) and
#       judged-and-never-selected (items judge it, and no tested round's scope has
#       selected one: the clause has no green run on record, and an item no scope
#       selected owes none — a round whose scope reaches one, which the human may order,
#       is what gives it evidence).
#
#       WHETHER THE LATEST ROUND RUNS AN ITEM, a pure function of the item's row and
#       that round's doors: an every-candidate item is selected, always; an in-scope
#       item is selected when one of the round's INCLUDED doors is one of its doors, or
#       comes from one of its registries — matched by the cell's text, exactly, as a
#       finding's door is.
#
#       WHAT CANNOT BE HELD IS NAMED. Of the four cells of an item's row that the
#       derivation reads, the clause is held to the census, and all four stand once a
#       round has run the item or owes it a run (item-set). ONE THING IS HELD BY NOTHING:
#       that an item's door is a door. A round's doors are derived afresh by its scope
#       step, so there is no list a door's text could be held to — and a door mistyped by
#       one letter reads exactly as a door the round's change did not reach. The item is
#       never selected, it owes nothing, and where another item of its clause is green the
#       clause is green and `next` can be `close` WITHOUT IT. That is A DECLARED BOUND of
#       this script, and no decision of it: nothing here can tell a mistyped door from one
#       the scoped design leaves out. What can be computed, is: `never_selected` and
#       `uncovered` name that silence from its two sides — the item no tested round
#       selected, and the door no item names — AND FORBID NOTHING, because an item a later
#       round leaves out was selected once and is not named, and an item the run's changes
#       never reach, a check that names no door and a door no instrument was built for
#       are the design ("hunt once per change, check always"). The names are for whoever
#       reads a stage's return, and for the human at every stop and at the close. A door
#       that turns out mistyped has its exit: the item has no run and no round owes it,
#       so item-set corrects its row — and the tested round that included the door then
#       owes it a run, which `next` asks for.
#
#       THE POSITION, a pure function of the rounds' records, the routes, what the
#       opening owes and the stop — the round, the cycle and the attempt are read here
#       and are never an argument of anybody's. With R the latest round:
#         the run is not ready   test refused · fix refused (not-ready)
#         no round yet           test {round 1, attempt 1} · fix refused (no-round)
#         R is not tested        test {round R, attempt: the highest test attempt on
#                                disk + 1} — a test stage that never reached its record
#                                is run again, as the next attempt
#                                · fix refused (not-tested)
#         R's outcome is set     test {round R+1, attempt 1} · fix refused (round-over)
#         R is tested, a finding test refused (round-open): fix it, rule on it, or drop
#         is open                the round · fix {round R, cycle: its recorded cycles
#                                + 1, attempt: the highest attempt of that cycle on
#                                disk + 1, land: false}
#                                — and where a finding is routed triage, THE STAGE THAT
#                                LEFT THE TRIAGE UNFINISHED carries `triage: true`: the
#                                fix stage (as above) when one has left a counted cycle
#                                or a report in R; else test {round R, attempt: the
#                                highest test attempt on disk + 1, triage: true} in
#                                place of its refusal. An invocation with `triage: true`
#                                finishes the round's triage and runs no instrument.
#         R is tested, nothing   test {round R+1, attempt 1} · fix {round R, cycle,
#         is open                attempt as above, land: whether a cycle is recorded} —
#                                the round's fix diff was audited and left nothing open
#         `next` is `retest`     test {round S, attempt: the highest test attempt of
#                                round S on disk + 1, rerun: {clause, round: S, items:
#                                [{item, attempt}], doors}} — THE RE-RUN: the first
#                                clause that is due, and of its due items those of the
#                                lowest round S that selected one, each with the attempt
#                                its run will be, and round S's included doors — the
#                                scope they run over again. An invocation with `rerun`
#                                runs those items and nothing else, inside round S: no
#                                round is begun, no scope resolved. · fix as above
#       Where the table says test {round R+1} and `next` is `stop` because the run stops
#       after every round, test is refused (stopped) instead: round R+1 waits for the
#       human's go. Where it says fix {…} and `next` is `stop` because a bound is spent,
#       fix is refused (round-bound, cycle-bound): one more fix round, or one more fix
#       cycle, is the human's to allow. And where a stage's attempts are spent (THE
#       ATTEMPTS OF A STAGE, above), its position is refused (attempts-spent). A
#       refusal is {"refused": WORD, "round": R}. The state is read from the working
#       tree, so it is the checked-out branch's: a fix stage's records ride its round
#       branch until the round lands.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record check-reports --run R --round N --stage test|fix [--cycle K]
#                        --attempt A [--] [REPORTER...]
#       Exactly one report of that attempt per launched reporter. Prints
#       {"check": "reports", "ok", "dir", "attempt", "missing", "extra",
#       "other_attempts", "reports"}: a launched reporter with no report is missing;
#       anything else in the stage's directory is extra, by file name — a report nobody
#       launched, a stray file, the temporary of a killed write. Both fail. Reports of
#       other attempts are listed and decide nothing. `reports` is each launched report
#       that is there, with the sha256 of its bytes: a check kept in an applied batch
#       says WHICH bytes it was a check of, and `vet` holds it to the disk again.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record check-ledger --run R [--] [KEY...]
#       Exactly one ledger row per finding id. Prints {"check": "ledger", "ok",
#       "missing", "duplicated"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record gate-set --run R --round N --commit SHA --summary FILE
#                        [--scratch DIR]...
#       THE CANDIDATE'S GATE, on record: what the full `dev/gate` printed on the commit
#       round N tests — FILE holds that run's whole output — as r<N>/gate.md: the commit,
#       the verdict, the totals line, and a row per step and per test the gate showed
#       red. FILE must hold ONE full gate's `GATE:` verdict line and its `tests   passed=`
#       totals line; a pre-check (`--fast`, `--quick`) prints not both and is refused
#       (bad-value). A RED gate must be the output of `dev/gate --keep-going`, which its
#       mode line says: a gate that stops at its first red stage names the first red and
#       not all of it, and is refused (bad-value). A green gate ran every test however it
#       was run. A round tests one candidate: another commit is refused (exists) — and
#       the candidate, where it is written, is held to this gate's commit (round-set):
#       the gate is written while the round is not tested, so before the candidate or
#       with it, and a record commit's gate is held to it as the candidate's (gate-check).
#       Prints {"gate", "commit", "verdict", "red"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record gate-check [--round N] --summary FILE [--scratch DIR]...
#       A RECORD COMMIT'S GATE, HELD TO THE CANDIDATE'S (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-06, "A
#       stabilization run's record commit under a red candidate"): FILE holds the output
#       of the full gate that ran on the tree WITH the records. The check holds when that
#       gate shows nothing red — no step, no test — that is not a row of r<N>/gate.md.
#       Without --round, or where round N holds no gate, nothing may be red: on a green
#       candidate this is the rule that a commit follows a green gate, unchanged. A test
#       step that failed and names no failing test never holds. Prints {"check": "gate",
#       "ok", "verdict", "totals", "red", "candidate", "known", "new"} — `new` names what
#       is red only with the records. BOTH GATES ARE RUN AS `dev/gate --keep-going`, and a
#       red one that was not is refused (bad-value, as under gate-set): no red step stops
#       such a gate but a red build, and its suite is ONE step, so what each names red is
#       all that is red, and the two are held against each other test by test. Where the
#       candidate's BUILD is red no test ran on either tree: what the records would turn
#       red is seen by the first gate of a candidate that builds.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record apply --run R [--round N] --subject TEXT [--scratch DIR]...
#                        <batch.json
#   dev/stabilize-record apply … --from FILE --sha256 HASH
#       A RECORD STEP'S WRITES, AS ONE BATCH. Stdin — or the file --from names, HELD TO
#       THE SHA256 ITS COMPOSER NAMES IT BY: a file of another hash is not the batch that
#       was composed, and is refused (bad-value) with the hash it has, nothing written;
#       the two flags come together or not at all (usage) — is a JSON array of calls, each
#       {"argv": [SUBCOMMAND, FLAG...], "stdin": TEXT}: the table writers above and the
#       two checks, exactly as they are called alone. They run in the batch's order, each
#       reading what the ones before it wrote, and NOTHING IS ON DISK until every call
#       was taken and every check among them holds; a refusal of one call, or a check
#       that does not hold (check), refuses the batch, naming the call. Then every file
#       the calls wrote is written as one batch (below), and the journal holds it as THE
#       APPLIED BATCH: its files with the sha256 each was written with and its text
#       before, the subject of the commit that records it, the round whose candidate
#       gate that commit is held to, how many calls and which checks it ran, and the act
#       it is a batch of (`record`). A run has one applied batch at a time (pending). A
#       BATCH THAT WOULD CHANGE NOTHING IS NO BATCH (no-change): where every file the
#       calls wrote is already what the run holds — the same ruling, sent twice — nothing
#       is written and no journal exists, because no commit could ever settle it. A
#       BATCH THAT HOLDS A STAGE'S REPORT
#       CHECK IS THE RECORD OF THAT ATTEMPT of the stage, and says so in the round's
#       record (round.md: recorded, as `test a<K>` or `fix-c<C> a<K>`) — which is what the
#       attempts that left no record are counted from (state). Prints {"applied", "files",
#       "results"} — one result per call, as that call prints it.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record item --run R --item I
#   dev/stabilize-record item-doors --run R --round N --item I
#   dev/stabilize-record untriaged --run R
#       WHAT AN AGENT OF A STAGE READS BY KEY (the second repair plan of the `test` half,
#       K9): each one line of JSON, each from the state as `state` reads it, each writing
#       nothing. The harness reads a digest of the state and no sentence of it
#       (`dev/stabilize-step`: THE DIGEST) — a line relayed through an agent does not
#       carry one — so a prompt names the read, and the agent runs it:
#         item         a row of the test set, whole, as the state's `items` has it — its
#                      brief in it: {"run", "item": {item, kind, clause, runs, doors,
#                      registries, brief, selected}}. An item the test set lacks is
#                      refused (no-such-row).
#         item-doors   the doors of round N's test set the item covers — its own doors,
#                      and those of its registries; all of the round's, for an item that
#                      runs on every candidate and names neither — each with its registry
#                      and its derivation: {"run", "round", "item", "doors": [{door,
#                      registry, derivation}]}. It reads THE ROUND IT IS ASKED FOR, which
#                      for a re-run is the round the position gives and not the latest;
#                      a round with no scope is refused (no-scope).
#         untriaged    the ledger's rows whose triage is not finished, whole and in the
#                      ledger's order, each with why (`ungraded`, `unverified`):
#                      {"run", "count", "untriaged": [...]} — the rows the state's
#                      `untriaged` names by key, and a stage's triage is handed.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record pending --run R
#       What of the run is written and in no commit yet, AS THIS SCRIPT KNOWS IT; writes
#       nothing. Prints {"run", "opened", "journal", "interrupted", "batch", "once",
#       "temporaries"}: whether a write was killed between its files — a batch being
#       placed, or one being taken back; the applied batch — {act, round, subject, calls,
#       checks, files: {path: sha256}, altered: the files of it whose content is no
#       longer what the batch wrote, discarding: that it is being taken back} — or null;
#       the files on disk that are written once and never rewritten: every report, and
#       each round's scope; and THIS SCRIPT'S OWN TEMPORARIES that a killed write left
#       (the read holds the shared lock, so none of them is a live writer's). Whether a
#       file is in a commit is git's to say, and this script reads no git:
#       dev/stabilize-step holds a tree to this answer, and has `recover` finish what
#       was killed before it does (its table of arrival states).
#
#   dev/stabilize-record discard --run R
#       Takes the applied batch back: every file of it as it was before the batch, or
#       gone where the batch made it, and the journal's batch with it. The reports and
#       the scope are no part of a batch and stay. IT IS A JOURNALED WRITE: the journal
#       first says that the batch is being taken back, so that one killed between two
#       files is finished by the next writer, and never read as applied. IT READS NO GIT,
#       and so takes a batch back whose commit is made: the act that asks git first is
#       `dev/stabilize-step discard`, which is the one an orchestrator calls. Prints
#       {"discarded"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record settle --run R
#       Forgets the applied batch, because the commit that records it was made: the one
#       caller is the step that made that commit (dev/stabilize-step record). Prints
#       {"settled"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record recover --run R
#       Settles a write that was killed, as every writer does before it writes (below):
#       a batch whose journal was written is put in place, a batch that was being taken
#       back is taken back, and the temporaries of a write killed before its journal are
#       removed. Prints {"recovered": the files it put in place, "discarded": the files
#       of a batch it finished taking back, "removed": how many temporaries}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record vet --run R [--subject TEXT] [--] [PATH...]
#       WHAT IS ABOUT TO BE COMMITTED, HELD TO WHAT THIS SCRIPT WOULD HAVE WRITTEN; writes
#       nothing. Every PATH is a file of the run, repository-relative as `pending` names
#       it, read from the disk and held to the rules a writer holds its text to (WHAT A
#       WRITER DOES TO ITS TEXT, below) by the writers' own functions: it lies below the
#       run's directory behind no link, a file beside the reports has a report's name,
#       and one beside the verdicts of the held checks a verdict's — and is the verdict
#       its result was written from (result-set); it is text; a report ends with its last line; NO HOST PATH IS LEFT IN IT —
#       the text is what the placeholder replacement leaves, so the public placeholders
#       themselves pass; no line reddens a standing fence; and the public-hygiene scan,
#       ONE scan over all of them and the subject. --subject is the subject of the commit
#       that would record them: one line, no host path, scanned with the files. And THE
#       APPLIED BATCH'S REPORT CHECKS ARE HELD TO THE DISK AGAIN: a report such a check
#       counted (check-reports: `reports`) is there, with the bytes it had — a check on
#       record never outlives the file it was a check of.
#       Prints {"check": "vet", "ok", "vetted": {path: sha256}, "refused": [{path, why,
#       detail}], "subject": null | {why, detail}, "stale": [{dir, attempt, gone,
#       changed}]} — `why` is the word a writer would have refused the text with (gone: no
#       such file), and a hit is named by where it is, never by what matched.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record vet --range [--] REV...
#       WHAT A PUSH WOULD PUBLISH, VETTED; writes nothing. REV... are the arguments of the
#       range as `git rev-list` takes them — a branch, `--not`, `--remotes=<remote>` — and
#       this is THE ONE CALL OF THIS SCRIPT THAT ASKS GIT ABOUT THE REPOSITORY, of the
#       commits of that range and of nothing else. Over the range: the two scanners CI
#       runs over a push, as CI runs them (.github/workflows/ci.yml, `hygiene`) — every
#       commit message and every added line, of every path. AND WHAT A COMMIT SAYS IS
#       HELD TO WHAT ITS FILES ARE HELD TO: of those two, gitleaks reads added lines and
#       never a message, so the messages of the range are scanned as the files of a
#       batch are (WHAT A WRITER DOES TO ITS TEXT, 4) — each a file of one throwaway
#       repository, under both scanners; a hit is the commit's, its `path` null (the
#       review of the core for three dangers, F6). AND A MERGE'S OWN LINES ARE READ BY
#       BOTH: the denylist scan asks git for what a merge itself introduced
#       (`--remerge-diff`), and here gitleaks is asked the same — one flag more than CI
#       passes it, which reads no merge's resolution. And for every commit that
#       changes a file of AN OPENED RUN (a path below completions/artifacts/<run>/ in a
#       commit that holds that run's opening.md), the rules above on each such file as
#       the commit holds it, and no host path in the commit's message. (What a commit
#       changes is, for a merge, what differs from every parent: what its parents
#       brought is vetted at their commits, or the remote holds it already.) A file outside a
#       run's directory is held to the two scanners and to nothing else: the placeholder
#       rule is the record's, and source code names /tmp.
#       Prints {"check": "vet", "ok", "range", "commits", "refused": [{commit, path, why,
#       detail}], "then"} — `path` is null for a commit's message, `then` what is done
#       about a commit that is refused: it is local, and rewriting it is the human's.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record set-aside --run R [--to DIR] [--] PATH...
#       A FILE THAT IS NO REPORT LEAVES THE TREE. Every PATH is one of the run's files
#       that are written once — a report, a round's scope — and is vetted as `vet` does;
#       each that is refused is MOVED to DIR, a directory outside the repository (one is
#       minted under the system temp directory where none is named), under its path with
#       `/` as `__`. A file that passes stays. The launched reporter whose file left has
#       then left no report, which `check-reports` says and a stage has a row for. A
#       table is never set aside: a batch is taken back whole (discard). Prints {"aside":
#       [{path, why, detail, to}], "vetted": {path: sha256}}.
#
# THE ONE WRITE, AND THE JOURNAL. Everything a call writes is ONE BATCH — one file for
# most calls, two for triage-set, every table of a record step for apply — and a batch is
# written whole or not at all: every text is fenced and scanned before any is written;
# each is written to a temporary beside its target; where the batch is more than one
# file the journal (.pending.json) then names which temporary becomes which file; each
# is put in place; and the journal forgets them. A call killed before the journal is
# written leaves no file of its batch, and its temporaries are removed by the next
# writer — and are no report to `check-reports`, which does not count them. A call killed
# after it leaves an INTERRUPTED write: `state` refuses to read
# across it (interrupted), and the next writer — or `recover` — puts the rest of the
# batch in place before it does anything else. TAKING A BATCH BACK (discard) is such a
# write too: the journal says it is under way before the first file is restored, and a
# killed one is finished the same way. A table has one writer at a time: every
# write is made under the run directory's lock, taken once a writer has its input and
# never while it waits for it, so rows written at once are all written, and state reads
# between two writes, never across one. Every JSON result is one line on stdout, printed
# once the call's batch has landed.
#
# A WRITER FED FROM A FILE (the second repair plan of the `test` half, K10; the human's
# ruling of 2026-10-07 that no step of a stage may need his permission). Three writers
# are called by an agent of a stage, and each used to be handed its text by a shell the
# agent composed — a here-document, or a redirect: a command no permission rule can
# name. With --from the agent writes the text to a file with its file tool, and the call
# is ONE plain invocation: `report` (its report), `scope-set` (the round's scope) and
# `apply` (a record's batch, with --sha256). The file is read as stdin would be — the
# same bytes give the same write, and every rule of the writer holds — and stdin is not
# read. A file that cannot be read is refused (bad-value). The other writers are called
# by nobody but a batch, which carries their text.
#
# WHERE THE STATE TAKES A WRITE — THE WRITER x STATE TABLE. A write is taken only where
# the state asks for it; anywhere else it is refused (bad-value: "the state does not ask
# for it") and nothing is written. The table is this script's own (WRITERS, ROUND_TAKES,
# RUN_TAKES): every subcommand has its row, and every fact of a round's record and of the
# run's, and a subcommand or a fact added without one is no call this script takes
# (fault). Each row, and why:
#   a round     report, scope-set, triage-set, result-set — and every fact of a round — are
#               taken for a round the run HAS BEGUN (its directory is there), and for the
#               ONE round the state hands a `test` stage (state: begins): after a round
#               that left nothing open, was dropped, or landed. Never past a stop nobody
#               lifted, past a round that is still open, while an item's re-run is what
#               is asked, or two rounds ahead: a round begun unasked would be the latest
#               round, and every stop is computed from the latest round. (While the
#               run's opening is not done no stage starts and nothing is decided, and the
#               opening's own order is not held: round 1 may be begun then.)
#               (result-set for a held check — its verdict's file, or `void` by a word
#               where it left none — is this row too, taken at the same positions: a
#               void by a word is the run of the test stage, or a re-run the state asks.)
#   untested    gate-set, and the facts candidate, base, binary, cross-model and
#               cross-model-void: as a round, and only while that round is not tested —
#               they are its test stage's record, written with or before the candidate.
#               (The candidate is held, where it is written, to the two writers that
#               take a commit for the round's evidence: the results' and the gate's.)
#   fix         the fact cycles: in the latest round, once it is tested, where the
#               position refuses no fix stage — not past either bound, not after the
#               round's outcome.
#   tested      the fact outcome: in the latest round, once it is tested — the human's
#               exit from it, at a bound too.
#   asked       the facts go, granted, reverify and again: only while the state asks the
#               human for exactly that (round-set).
#   once        the run's stop mode, previous release, default scope and clauses: where
#               the run does not hold them yet, and then never again.
#   raised      the run's two bounds: at the opening, and then only at the stop that
#               names the bound.
#   counted     ran, awaiting, recorded and the run's findings: never from a caller.
#   anywhere    ledger-add, ledger-set, bound-set, item-set: the ledger, the declared
#               bounds and the test set are held to no position. A finding is added by
#               whichever stage finds it, and at the opening; a disposition is a fixer's
#               record or the human's ruling — on a fork too, which the route asks the
#               human for; a bound is the human's at the opening and at every list;
#               an instrument that is built later takes its row when it is built. What
#               such a write CAN do is add work or record a ruling — it lifts no stop.
#               (No POSITION holds item-set; what the run holds does: the census, and
#               what a round has run of an item or owes it — item-set.)
#
# EVERY LOOP HAS A COUNTER ON RECORD AND AN EXIT TO THE HUMAN. Of the nine values of `next`
# five hand over — not-ready, rule, unsettled, close, stop — and four are steps a stage
# takes without asking: test, triage, fix, retest. A stage that takes one either REACHES
# ITS RECORD, and then one of these counters moved, or it does not, and then its attempt
# is counted. So every pair of steps that can follow each other without the human is
# bounded by what the step it ARRIVES at counts:
#   … -> test    a round is begun only after one that is over, and with a finding open a
#                round is over only by the human's exit (outcome); the rounds that have a
#                fix stage are counted (fix_rounds) and bounded by the bound across rounds,
#                where the run stops (round-bound) — or the run stops after every round
#   … -> triage  the triage records that left a finding unsettled are counted per finding
#                (awaiting): two, then the finding is the human's (…-after-retry)
#   … -> fix     the fix cycles of the round are counted (cycles) and bounded by the bound
#                inside a round, where the run stops (cycle-bound)
#   … -> retest  an item's runs in its round are counted (results, ran): two, then its
#                clause is the human's (human_clauses)
#   and x -> x without a record, for each of the four: the attempts of the stage that
#                left no record are counted (unrecorded): two, then the stage is the
#                human's (human_stages).
# A step that is the human's becomes a stage's again only by a fact the human's ruling
# writes: a go, a raised bound, a disposition, or one more — re-run, triage, attempt —
# each of which allows exactly one.
# ONE RECORD MOVES NO COUNTER, and is not bounded here: a fix stage's record that writes a
# fix and counts no cycle (a cycle that was not audited). `cycles` is the one fact that
# says a fix stage worked, and what a fix cycle leaves on record is the `fix` half's to
# rebuild (the repair plan's change C2); that stage refuses to start until it is.
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT CANNOT KNOW: ITS CALLER. It is a command, and whoever can run it can
# make any call of it. What it guarantees of a call is its shape, its vocabulary, that a
# fact written once stands, that a table has one writer at a time — and that the state
# takes the write where it stands (the table above). It does NOT know
# that a human ruled, nor that an item ran:
# `admitted`, `bound` and `later` and a declared bound's row are taken from any caller,
# at any time, on any row, any number of times,
# and `ledger-set` with `open` takes a ruling back. THAT THE HUMAN'S RULINGS REACH THE
# RECORD ONLY AS THE HUMAN'S IS HELD ELSEWHERE: the harness composes those calls in ONE
# function, from the `rulings` argument of an invocation and from nothing else
# (.claude/workflows/stabilize.js, `rulingsRecordPrompt`), and a fence holds that no
# other code of it spells them (tooling-tests/stabilize_harness_fence.rs, arms f and k).
# Outside the harness the confinement is the orchestrator's discipline, and nothing's
# enforcement. Nor does it know git: `settle` trusts that the commit was made, `discard`
# that it was not, and whether a pending file is committed is dev/stabilize-step's to
# read — which is why that tool, and no caller of this script, decides between the two
# (its `git-state`, `record` and `discard`). (ONE read asks
# git, and only about the commits it is handed: `vet --range`.)
#
# AND IT CANNOT KNOW WHO WROTE A FILE. A reporter has a shell, so a file at a report's
# path is whatever its last writer left — the text this script refused, written there by
# a redirect, has a report's name. So what a writer checks at its write is checked AGAIN
# WHERE THE CONTENT BECOMES PERMANENT, by `vet`, which dev/stabilize-step asks at three
# boundaries: every report check (the file is no report, and leaves the tree: set-aside),
# the record's commit (nothing is committed, and the batch is taken back), and every push
# it makes (nothing is pushed). A push made by hand with plain git is outside this.
#
# What a writer does to its text, in order — every writer alike, because every write
# goes through one function:
#
#   1. Host paths become the public placeholders. A path into this repository becomes
#      its repository-relative form (`.` for the root); a path under a --scratch root
#      becomes <scratch>/…; under the system temp directory ($TMPDIR, /tmp, /var/tmp,
#      macOS's /var/folders/*/*/T), <tmp>/…; under a home directory (this machine's,
#      and any /Users/<name> or /home/<name>), ~/…. The longest root wins. A session's
#      scratch area nobody declared still names the home directory, flattened into a
#      path segment, after that: it is refused (host-path) with what to pass.
#   2. In a table, a cell cannot break its row: `|` is escaped, a line break becomes
#      <br>, an empty cell is `-`.
#   3. No file that would redden a standing fence. One fence reads every markdown file,
#      untracked ones included — install_line (crates/cli/tests/install_line.rs): a
#      file with a line that starts `cargo install jigc` is refused (fence), naming it.
#      Reports stay markdown; write the command inside a line, or behind a `$ `.
#   4. The public-hygiene scan, hard (implementation/public-hygiene.md). The files of
#      the call's batch are committed, each at its own path, into ONE throwaway
#      repository, and the two scanners CI runs over a push run over that commit — once
#      per batch, however many files it holds: dev/hygiene-scan, unmodified, with the
#      private denylist ($JIGC_DENYLIST_FILE, else ~/.config/jigc/denylist), and
#      gitleaks with this repository's .gitleaks.toml. A hit is refused (hygiene), by
#      where it is and never by what matched. A scanner that could not run — no
#      denylist, no gitleaks, a pattern no matcher accepts, a scanner error — DID NOT
#      RUN (did-not-run): that is never a pass, and nothing is written.
#   5. The write is atomic: a temporary beside the target, then a link (a report, which
#      never replaces a file) or a rename (a table) — and, for a batch of more than one
#      file, journaled (THE ONE WRITE, above). A run killed at any point leaves no
#      partial file at the target.
#
# WHAT IT IS HANDED NEVER ENDS IN A TRACEBACK. A JSON body nested past what the
# interpreter parses, a text JSON can spell and no file can hold (a lone surrogate), an
# argument that is not UTF-8, and a table cell a hand turned into something that only
# looks like a number are each a refusal of the class they belong to (bad-value,
# corrupt). And what no refusal names is still one line and a status of its own (fault),
# with the line it was raised at.
#
# Exit status, and the word a refusal opens with — `stabilize-record: refused <word>:
# …`, one line on stderr, nothing on stdout:
#
#    0  written, the check holds, or the state is printed
#    2  usage         an unknown subcommand or flag, a missing argument
#    3  not-opened    the run has no opening record
#    4  bad-id        an identifier outside its grammar
#    5  outside       a link below the run directory: the write would land outside it
#    6  exists        the report's path is taken — pass the next attempt; or a round's
#                     scope, a fact of its record, the run's stop mode, previous
#                     release, default scope or clauses are written once and stand; or
#                     an item has its result in a round that is not tested yet; or an
#                     item a round has run or owes a run keeps its clause, runs, doors
#                     and registries
#    7  bad-value     a value outside its vocabulary, a malformed row, a report that is
#                     not text; a file named by --from that cannot be read, or that is
#                     not the file --sha256 names; an outcome word for a held check —
#                     green or red, or a void whose reason is no word of its shape — or a
#                     verdict's file that is not the tool's verdict of that check on that
#                     commit — or A WRITE THE STATE DOES NOT ASK FOR: a round that is not
#                     begun and not handed out, a fact outside its position, a go, a
#                     grant, a raised bound or the re-run of an item nobody is asked
#                     for; a result for an item the round's scope does not select; an
#                     item of a clause the census does not name, or a census that does
#                     not name a clause an item judges
#    8  no-such-row   ledger-set names a key the ledger lacks, or result-set an item the
#                     test set lacks
#    9  fence         the file would redden a standing fence, which is named
#   10  host-path     a host path the placeholders could not replace
#   11  hygiene       the public-hygiene scan found a hit
#   12  did-not-run   a scanner could not run
#   13  corrupt       a table is not the one this script writes, or holds a cell it
#                     does not write — the clause table too, which is the one the item
#                     results give or is refused — or lacks a row, a result or a report
#                     that a record names: what is deleted is missed
#   14  no-such-bound out-of-scope cites a bound the declared-bounds list lacks
#   15  no-scope      triage-set, result-set or a round's candidate names a round whose
#                     scope was never written
#   16  truncated     the report does not end with its last line: it was cut off
#   17  interrupted   a write was killed between its files: the state is not read across
#                     it, and the next writer — or `recover` — finishes it
#   18  pending       a batch is applied and not committed: a second one is not applied
#   19  check         a check inside a batch does not hold: the batch is not written
#   20  check-reports failed: a report is missing or extra (the result is on stdout)
#   21  check-ledger  failed: a row is missing or duplicated (the result is on stdout)
#   22  gate-check    failed: the gate shows something red that the candidate's gate
#                     did not (the result is on stdout)
#   23  fault         an input no refusal names, or a defect of this script: one line
#   24  vet           failed: a file, a subject or a commit is not what this script would
#                     have written, or a report check is stale (the result is on stdout)
#   25  no-change     a batch every file of which is what the run holds already: there
#                     is nothing to record, and nothing was written
#
# Needs python3, git and bash (dev/hygiene-scan), which the gate needs already, and
# gitleaks and a denylist to write anything at all.
# tooling-tests/dev_stabilize_record.rs drives it.

